00:00:01.670 --> 00:00:03.439 align:start position:0%
the public's nostalgia for retro  consoles has been growing over the last  several years  Nintendo kicked off a wave of  plug-and-play style systems with 2016's  Nest classic edition and followed up  with last year's snus classic now Sony  is looking to throw their hat in the  ring with the PlayStation Classic coming  next month  we got a hands-on look with this system  and want to give you our early  impressions let's start with the  hardware if you own a Ness or cest  Classic the PlayStation Classic will be  very familiar to you it's a miniature  version of the original system featuring  an HDMI port and micro USB for power in  the back and slots for two controllers  in the front the system's physical three  buttons all work as well the power does  well what you expect  turn the system on and off while the  reset button works the same as with  Nintendo systems bringing you back to  the main menu the interesting one is the  disk open button this is used when a  game needs you to switch discs it's  pretty cool that they found a way to  emulate the physical act of switching  the discs rather than having it happen  automatically instead of a proprietary  port the controllers plug in through USB  Sony said it doesn't feature support for  other controllers to be plugged in or  for the controllers to work on PC  although I figure some smart people will  figure that one out  the controller's themselves are based on  the original systems controllers that  means no analog sticks or Rumble support  we brought an original controller with  us for comparison and the classics  version feels very faithful it's just a  touch lighter in weight and the buttons  feel more clicky that's also probably  because we are comparing them to a 20  year old controller that seen lots of  use while I somewhat admire wanting to  stick to the nostalgia of the original  hardware I don't think anyone's gonna  argue that the Dual Shock wasn't a major  improvement over the original design and  it's disappointing that we won't have  the option to play with analog sticks or  Rumble in fact without them it will be  impossible to appreciate iconic moments  like the Psycho Mantis fight in Metal  Gear Solid  the menu is designed around the original  Playstations menus sporting a cool blue  background and that memorable rainbow  paint splatter behind the text when it  comes to settings the classic is very  bare-bones the system runs at 720p with  no options for resolution change or  adjustments to aspect ratio  there's no borders as well and no  filters such as scan lines while I  personally never use scan lines  preferring a sharper look there are  elements of menus and text of these  games that have an age gracefully and  scan lines could help make them look  less ugly the PlayStation Classic is  designed to just plug in and work with  no fuss but if you're looking for a bit  more control in your presentation you're  not going to get it here when you quit  out of a game a safe point is  automatically created that you can then  resume unlike the Ness and sus classic  there was only one save slot in each  time you reset you're asked if you want  to overwrite that save  there are however internal memory cards  that function just like the original  system they even use the same game icons  which is a great little touch the  PlayStation Classic comes with 20 games  with over 2,500 games released for the  system there was always gonna be some  personal favourites left out on the list  but even with that in mind we can't help  but be disappointed in the selection  missing iconic games like crash  bandicoot Symphony of the Night and tomb  raider games like Destruction Derby and  cool borders - are fine but not really  something we're itching to play of  course there are big names like Metal  Gear Solid Final Fantasy 7 twisted metal  Resident Evil director's cut and Tekken  3 there are also some neat cult classics  like odd world Abe's Oddysee  super Puzzle Fighter 2 turbo and Wild  Arms I think it's cool a game like  revelations persona is in the collection  even though I don't think the game has  aged all that well in fact many of these  early 3d games have an aged well and  your nostalgia is gonna be a big factor  in your enjoyment for games like Rainbow  six or cool borders too  as far as the technical side of how  these games are performing in our brief  time they seem to run as well as we  remember polygamous scaled on a modern  TV the same can't be said for certain UI  in other static images the rendered  backgrounds and Resident Evil suffer and  text and Ridge Racer is so blown out it  can be hard to read this isn't really a  fault of the PlayStation classic but  rather further evidence of how poor some  of these games have aged again this is a  case where a scanline filter might have  helped out unfortunately we couldn't do  any direct capture ourselves preventing  us from doing any actual comparisons to  original hardware overall our early  impressions are pretty mixed the  PlayStation Classic does exactly what it  intends letting you play these games at  a sharp 720p with a simple setup and no  fuss but the product feels a bit  bare-bones with no visual customization  and controllers that lack analog sticks  and Rumble support the game selection  certainly doesn't feel like the greatest  hits of the console the way the nests  and senesce classics did we'll be  putting the playstation classic through  its full paces next month when it  launches on December 3rd  [Music]
 

